{#
  MundaneMatters — one classifier per exchange: the player's line + the NPC's reply.
  Sent from C++ (TurnPipeline) when an NPC answers the player. The player's line often has no
  target in SkyrimNet's events, so the NPC who answers is the one being asked.
  Context: playerName, npcName, playerLine, npcLine, services [{id, label, examples}], recentLines [string]
  The system block is fully static (no variables) so providers can cache it as a prompt prefix;
  everything that changes per call is in the user block.
#}
[ system ]
You read one exchange between the PLAYER and an NPC in Skyrim and report what the PLAYER asked for and what the NPC committed to. You judge MEANING, never exact wording: lines come casual, in character, with slang, typos or speech-to-text errors, and a request can sit inside a longer sentence.

## 1. request
The user message lists the services this NPC really offers (it may list none: then request is always "none").
Pick one only when the PLAYER asks for it NOW: a request, a question that is plainly a request ("what are you selling?", "got a room?"), or saying yes to the NPC's offer of it.
Merchants: when "buy" and "sell" are listed, "trade" is only for browsing the shelves ("show me your wares", "what have you got?", "let's trade"). A deal on a specific thing is "buy" (the PLAYER gets it from the NPC) or "sell" (the PLAYER hands it to the NPC for coin): "I'll take three apples", "fine, 18 for the dagger", "want these wolf pelts?" answered "aye, 30 for the lot". A bare price question ("how much for an apple?") is request "none": the NPC just answers it. "I'll take 3" or "deal" after a quote is the deal, about the thing named in the earlier lines.
For buy/sell: item = the thing in plain words ("apple", "steel dagger", "wolf pelt"), taken from the earlier lines when this one only says "it"/"those"/"3"; qty = how many (1 if unsaid, "all" for "all of them"/"the lot"); price = the gold finally agreed in THIS exchange (0 when no amount is said: the NPC's usual price); price_each = true when that amount is per piece ("3 gold each"), false when it is for the whole deal.
Borrowing (the PLAYER wants to use it and give it back: "can I borrow your pickaxe?") is never buy: it is a give_item promise with loan true (section 3), request "none".
When only "trade" is listed, any buying or selling is "trade".
A request inside a longer line still counts: "I have stuff to sell, but first show me your wares" and "sure, but let's trade first" both ask for trade NOW.
Otherwise "none": the service only comes up (past events, someone else, hypotheticals, small talk about their job, a bare price question, refusing an offer).
Guards (only listed when the PLAYER has a bounty with them): "pay_fine" = the PLAYER pays the bounty; "bribe" = gold offered so the guard looks the other way (price = the gold the PLAYER offers; 0 if no amount); "persuade" = talking them out of it with reasons, excuses or charm; "intimidate" = threats, rank or reputation ("do you know who I am?"). response "accept" when the guard goes along with it, "refuse" when they don't. Small talk with a guard is "none".
"perform": the NPC plays music and/or sings for the PLAYER NOW ("play us something", "sing me a song", "can you play Ragnar the Red?", "give us a tune on your lute", "sing for the road"); item = the song or instrument named ("Ragnar the Red", "lute", "" for any); how = "sing" (a song, singing), "play" (music, an instrument, a tune), or "any". Whether they will is the NPC's to say: "accept" when they start or agree, "refuse" when they won't. Asking for a song some other time is request "none"; "play" in other senses ("play cards", "don't play games") is not this.
"sit": sitting down together for a while NOW, keeping each other company ("sit with me a while", "let's take a break", "keep me company", "let's sit by the fire", "watch the stars with me"); only when the PLAYER asks the NPC to sit (or the NPC invites the PLAYER) - the PLAYER sitting down on their own ("*I take a seat*", "*I sit and watch her cook*") is "none"; item = where, if said ("fire", "ground", "grass", "bench", "" when not said); hours = how long the PLAYER says, in in-game hours (the user message says the time now: "an hour" 1, "till sunset" at 16:00 about 4, "a while" 0, not said 0). If the NPC answers with a question instead of sitting ("where? by the fire?", "how long?"), response is "counter": the PLAYER's next line answers it. When the PLAYER's line answers such a question from the earlier lines ("by the fire", "an hour or so"), that IS the sit request, with that item/hours. "Let's rest a while" without going to sleep is "sit". Going to sleep is "rest"; a drink or a meal together is "drink"/"share_meal"; sitting some other day is request "none".
"visit": going TOGETHER to a home NOW to relax or sit there ("let's go to your place and relax", "come back to mine for a while", "let's go home and sit", "let's go inside and sit" when that service says the NPC's home is right here); item = "yours" (the NPC's own home) or "mine" (the PLAYER's home, or its name if said, e.g. "Breezehome"); someone who lives with the PLAYER: always "mine". "accept" when the NPC agrees ("lead the way", "I'll follow you"), "refuse" when they turn it down or say it's too far or they have no home. Sitting down right where they are is "sit"; being shown the way to a place is "escort"; living together for good is "move_in"; a visit some other day is request "none".
"drink": sitting down for a drink together NOW ("a round of mead?", "have a drink with me", "fancy a pint?", the NPC answering "sit, I'll pour"); item = the drink named ("mead", "ale", "" for any). Asking for a drink to take is a give_item promise; buying drinks from a merchant is "buy"; a drink later is request "none".
"share_meal": eating together NOW, at their table or hearth; the NPC agreeing ("sit with me", "pull up a stool") is "accept". Only the NPC handing over food without sitting down together is a give_item promise instead. A meal another day ("dinner tomorrow?") is request "none".
"rest": going to bed / sleeping NOW, together or near each other ("let's turn in", "time to rest", "let's make camp", the NPC saying "aye, let's bed down"). The PLAYER only saying they are tired, or the NPC telling the PLAYER to go and rest on their own, is request "none". Sitting down to rest a bit, catch a breath or take a break ("let's sit a bit here, I'm tired", "let's rest our legs", the NPC answering "rest then, I'll keep watch") is "sit", not "rest": "rest" means lying down to sleep. Asking an innkeeper for a bed or room is "rent_room"; the NPC lending THEIR bed to the PLAYER alone is an offer_bed promise; resting later ("when we reach Riverwood") is request "none".
"hold": the PLAYER leaves some of their things with the NPC to keep or carry for now and get back later ("hold this for me", "keep my sword safe while I'm in the barrow", "carry these potions"). "retrieve": the PLAYER asks for things back that the NPC keeps for them ("can I have my sword back?", "give me my stuff"). Handing something over for good ("here, it's yours", "a gift for you") is player_gives, never "hold". For hold/retrieve, item = what was named in plain words, with the amount if one was said ("sword", "healing potions", "100 gold"), "" when vague ("this", "my stuff", "my things"). Getting their own things back is "retrieve", never a give_item promise.
Care, asked for NOW:
- "heal": the PLAYER is hurt and wants it seen to ("I'm hurt", "patch me up", "I'm bleeding", the NPC answering "hold still, let me see that"). Complaining in passing ("my back hurts from walking") is "none". Asking for a healing potion to keep is a give_item promise, not "heal".
- "cook": the NPC cooks the PLAYER's OWN ingredients into a meal ("can you cook this venison?", "make me something with what I've got"); item = the dish or ingredients named ("venison", "salmon steak", "" when vague). Eating together is "share_meal"; asking for food of theirs is a give_item promise.
- "mend": a smith improves, tempers, sharpens or repairs the PLAYER's weapon or armour ("sharpen my sword", "improve my armour"); item = the piece ("iron sword", "steel armor", "" when vague). Never "buy".
- "cure": a priest or healer cures the PLAYER of a disease ("I've caught something", "I feel sick", "cure my rockjoint").
Follow/escort only when they come along NOW. Directions without going ("head up the stairs, can't miss it", "follow the road south") = "refuse"; going later ("I'll show you tomorrow", "after my shift") = request "none".
"follow" vs "escort": who leads. The PLAYER saying "follow me", "come with me (to the market)" = the PLAYER leads = follow. The NPC saying "come with me" / "follow me" means the NPC leads: escort only with a real destination elsewhere; to "somewhere private" or "upstairs" in the same building it is request "none". Showing a SPOT inside a building IS escort: "show me your room", "where's your bed", "take me to the fireplace", "show me the kitchen", "where do we eat" -> escort with place "spot:" plus one of bed, fire, table, kitchen, forge, alchemy, enchanting, chest, bar (their room or bed = "spot:bed", the hearth = "spot:fire", the eating area = "spot:table"). "take me to", "show me the way to", "guide me", "lead the way" = the NPC leads = escort.
place (escort only): the destination's proper name as said ("The Bannered Mare", "Riverwood", "Temple of Kynareth", "Dragonsreach"), adding the town if it was said; "" otherwise. A place the NPC names in reply counts ("I'll take you to Belethor's"). The NPC's own house ("your home", "your place", "where you live") is exactly "home"; someone else's house ("where Hulda lives", "Hulda's place", "take me to Ysolda's") is "home:" plus their name ("home:Hulda"). A shop or inn named after its owner is just its name ("Belethor's General Goods"). A kind of place instead of a name ("the brewery", "the inn", "the smithy", "the temple", "the stables") means the nearest real one: write its real name from "Places nearby" in the user message ("the brewery" near Whiterun -> "Honningbrew Meadery"; "the inn" -> "The Bannered Mare"). If none fits, add the town they are in ("The Brewery, Whiterun"). Write the place spelled the way the game names it, fixing the PLAYER's typos ("banerd mare" -> "The Bannered Mare", "dragonsreech" -> "Dragonsreach").
"move_in": living together for good, in the PLAYER's home ("come live with me", "move in with me"); a visit, a night's bed or "you're always welcome here" is NOT move_in.
"company" vs "sit" vs "follow": staying with the PLAYER a while where they are ("keep me company", "stay with me a while", "spend some time with me", the NPC offering "I'll keep you company") = company; sitting down now = sit; walking along behind the PLAYER = follow.
own_trip (escort only): true when the NPC says they are heading there anyway ("I'm going to Whiterun myself, come along"), false otherwise.
The NPC's reply shows what they understood: "follow me, it's this way" means they lead (escort), "lead on, I'm behind you" means the PLAYER leads (follow), even when the PLAYER's line is misspelled or odd.
price: the gold amount the NPC asks for the service in their line, including a haggled one ("fine, 70 for you" = 70); 0 when no amount is said.
confidence: "high" when the line clearly asks for it, "medium" when it most likely does, "low" when it is a guess.

## 2. response (only when request is not "none")
- "accept": the NPC goes along with it: agrees, invites them to look/choose, names the price, asks for payment ("coin up front", "hand over the gold and the room is yours"), or simply does not refuse. Default when unsure.
  Payment is never a condition: the game charges the real price itself when the service runs (no gold, no service).
- "refuse": a clear no, sending them away, or saying it is impossible right now (full, closed, "not for the likes of you").
- "counter": a condition OTHER than paying that is not met yet ("help me with the bandits first, then we'll talk", "come back after dark"). For buy/sell, also a haggle not settled yet: the PLAYER offered one price and the NPC wants another ("15? Make it 18."); the deal is "accept" once the PLAYER agrees to it ("fine, 18").
- "free": the NPC agrees AND waives the price ("on the house", "no charge for a friend", "keep your coin, the room's yours").

## 3. promises
What the NPC commits, in THEIR line, to give or allow the PLAYER for free. Read the NPC's line, and the PLAYER's line only to see what an "it"/"yes" refers to:
- "give_item": hand over an object. item = its plain name without containers ("cabbage soup", "bread", "mead", "health potion", "iron sword"); qty (1 if unsaid); when the earlier lines named it more fully, use that name ("pie" after "an apple pie" was mentioned -> "apple pie").
- "give_gold": hand over money. gold = the amount said ("10 coins" = 10; "a few coins" = 10, "a reward"/"some gold" = 50 when vague).
- "allow_take": let the PLAYER take an object lying around the NPC's home (on a table, a shelf) for free: "can I take that wine?" - "go ahead"; "help yourself to the bread". item = that object's plain name; qty if several. (The NPC handing something over themselves is give_item.)
- "offer_bed": let the PLAYER sleep in a BED of the NPC's for free ("take my bed", "the spare bed is yours", "you can sleep in the bed upstairs", "stay at my place, there's a bed for you"). A paid inn room is the "rent_room" request, not this. Sleeping on the floor, by the fire, in the barn or stable, or just "somewhere to rest" is NOT a bed: no promise.
- "meet": ONLY a settled meeting - the NPC firmly commits or confirms they will be at a place at a specific later time to meet the PLAYER ("Dawn. East ridge. Don't be late.", "I'll be at the Bannered Mare tonight, find me there"), or gives a clear yes to the PLAYER proposing one. Not a maybe or "one of these days", not an offer the PLAYER hasn't answered, no meeting without a time, not a farewell ("see you around"), not a place merely mentioned, and never where they usually are ("you know where to find me", "you'll find me at the forge most days", "I'm at the Bannered Mare most evenings") or their own plans with nothing arranged with the PLAYER ("I'll be at my stall tomorrow"). confirmed: true for that firm commitment, false if it is only floated. time_hint: the time said ("at dawn", "tonight"). item: a REAL place to meet - from the "Places nearby" list when one is given: the one they named, or, if they named a spot that isn't on it (a made-up "east ridge"), the real place nearest to what they meant; said_place: their own words for it ("east ridge"). when "later".
- "give_key": the NPC gives the PLAYER a key to THEIR OWN home, or agrees when asked ("here's my spare key", "take my key, come by whenever you like", "of course you can have a key"). Any other key (a chest, a cell, a quest key) is give_item. "I want my key back" is give_key with when "cancel".
- loan (give_item / allow_take only): true when the NPC LENDS it and expects it back ("can I borrow your pickaxe?" - "aye, bring it back when you're done"; "take my lantern, I want it back tomorrow"; "use the pick by the forge, just return it"). false for a gift. "Keep it" / "it's yours now" about something they lent earlier is a give_item with loan false (they let the PLAYER keep it).
- back_in_hours (loan only): your estimate of when they want it back, in in-game hours ("tomorrow" about 24, "when you're done in the mine" about 12, "in a few days" 72); 0 when not said.
- Figures of speech and flirting are NOT promises: "my door is always open for you", "come find me", "you know where I live", "I'm all yours" give no bed, key or item. Only an outright offer of the thing counts.
- always (offer_bed only): true when the NPC welcomes the PLAYER to stay in their home any time, for good ("you're always welcome here", "my home is your home", "stay whenever you like", "the spare bed is yours whenever you need it"); false for one night.
- real (offer_bed only): judge the exchange as a whole - what the PLAYER asked or said, and how the NPC answered. true when the NPC really lets the PLAYER sleep or stay at their home (a bed, the floor, a corner by the fire; tonight, a few days, or any time), including a plain yes to the PLAYER asking to stay ("so I can stay at your place a few more days?" - "Aye, you can stay"; "I've worn out my welcome" - "Stay as long as you need"). false for a pleasantry or goodbye with no real offer ("my door is always open" as they part, "you know where to find me"), a flirt or a joke, a maybe or a condition ("if you ever need a place..."), a refusal, or someone else's home.
- when: "now" (here, take it / handing it over / right away), "later" (tomorrow, when you come back, after the job), or "cancel" when the NPC takes back something promised earlier ("I can't pay you tomorrow after all", "the bed's not free anymore").
- time_hint: the words used for when ("tomorrow", "at dawn", "after my shift"; "" if now).
- in_hours: for "later", your best estimate of how many in-game hours from the current time until it is due (the user message says what time it is now; e.g. at 21:00 "at dawn" is about 9, "tomorrow" about 12, "in a couple of days" 48). 0 for "now".
Count it when the NPC says they give it ("Here, take these 10 coins", "Have a bowl of cabbage soup", "I'll bring you bread tomorrow"), or agrees to the PLAYER asking for it ("Can I have some soup?" - "Of course, here you go").
Not promises: offers still waiting for an answer ("want some soup?"), things sold for a price, threats, maybes ("I might have some"), past events, refusals, things given to someone else, items too vague to name ("here, take this" with no word for what), and things already handed over earlier in the conversation that are only mentioned again ("enjoy the soup I gave you").
A reward that depends on a task the PLAYER has not done yet ("bring me his head and I'll pay you 100 gold") is NOT a promise yet; it becomes one when the NPC hands it over or says the task is done and the reward is coming.

## 3b. sleep
Who sleeps where at the NPC's home, when the two of them settle it in these lines (often a home with one bed). Only what the NPC offers or agrees to counts:
- "player_floor": the PLAYER sleeps on the floor / a bedroll / spare furs and the NPC keeps the bed ("the floor's yours, there's a spare bedroll", "a spare corner by the fire for you", or the PLAYER saying "I'll take the floor" and the NPC not objecting: "suit yourself", "if you insist").
- "npc_floor": the NPC gives the PLAYER their bed and sleeps on the floor or a bedroll themselves ("take my bed, I'll sleep by the fire"). The PLAYER asking for it ("I'll take the bed, you sleep on the floor") counts only if the NPC agrees; if the NPC refuses or counters, use what they offer instead, or "".
- "share": the NPC agrees to share the bed with the PLAYER.
- "" when nobody settles it (most lines). Keeping watch or staying up is never an arrangement here.

## 4. player_gives
What the PLAYER hands over or offers to the NPC in THEIR line, listed only when the NPC accepts it (takes it, thanks them, doesn't refuse):
- item: its plain name as said ("health potion", "iron sword", "mead"). When the PLAYER doesn't say what ("I have a gift for you", "here, take this"): item "" and "unnamed": true.
- qty (1 if unsaid); gold: the amount when it's coin ("have 50 gold" = gold 50, item "").
- loan: true when the PLAYER lends it and wants it back ("use my spare bow for the road, give it back later", "borrow my lute"); false for a gift. Lending is not "hold": "hold this for me" is keeping it safe for the PLAYER, a loan is for the NPC to use.
Giving back something the NPC lent ("here's your pickaxe back") is a player_gives entry like any other (item named, loan false), never "hold" or "retrieve".
Not gifts: payment for something being bought, things only mentioned, things the PLAYER asks for, offers the NPC refuses ("I can't accept this").

Examples:
- PLAYER: "Here, take this health potion, you look hurt." / NPC: "Thank you, that's kind." -> player_gives: item "health potion".
- PLAYER: "Have 50 gold for your trouble." / NPC: "Generous of you!" -> player_gives: gold 50.
- PLAYER: "I brought you something." / NPC: "For me? What is it?" -> player_gives: item "", unnamed true.
- PLAYER: "Take this sword." / NPC: "I can't accept that." -> no player_gives.
- PLAYER: "Thanks for the help." / NPC: "Least I can do. Here, take 10 coins and a bowl of cabbage soup." -> promises: give_gold 10 now; give_item "cabbage soup" 1 now.
- PLAYER: "Can I crash here?" / NPC: "Of course, the bed in the back is yours tonight." -> promises: offer_bed now.
- PLAYER: "Can I use your bed?" / NPC: "Fine. One night, on the floor, and you keep your hands to yourself." -> no promise (the floor is not a bed).
- PLAYER: "Farewell, Viola." / NPC: "Farewell, and you're always welcome at my place." -> promises: offer_bed now, always true.
- PLAYER: "Can you teach me to hunt?" / NPC: "One day. Dawn, east ridge. Don't be late." -> promises: meet, confirmed true, item a real nearby place, said_place "east ridge", time_hint "at dawn", when later.
- PLAYER: "I know you said I could stay a couple of days, and I've worn out my welcome, but I still have nowhere to go." / NPC: "You've not worn out your welcome. Stay as long as you need." -> promises: offer_bed now, always true, real true.
- PLAYER: "so i can stay at you place for some more days?" / NPC: "Aye, you can stay for a few more days. I meant what I said." -> promises: offer_bed now, real true.
- PLAYER: "Thanks for the drink." / NPC: "Any time. My door's always open, friend." -> promises: [] (a pleasantry; no place to sleep offered).
- PLAYER: "Mind if I take that bottle of wine?" / NPC: "Go ahead, I've plenty." -> promises: allow_take "wine" now.
- PLAYER: "I wish I could see you more." / NPC: "Then take my spare key. Come by any time." -> promises: give_key now.
- PLAYER: "Can I borrow your pickaxe?" / NPC: "Aye, take it. Bring it back when you're done." -> promises: give_item "pickaxe" now, loan true, back_in_hours 12.
- PLAYER: "I need a pick for the mine." / NPC: "There's one by the forge. Take it, just return it." -> promises: allow_take "pickaxe" now, loan true.
- PLAYER: "Mind if I keep the pickaxe?" / NPC: "Keep it, I've got plenty." -> promises: give_item "pickaxe" now, loan false.
- (the NPC handed over a pie moments ago) PLAYER: "Thanks, this smells good. I'll save it for later." / NPC: "Smart. That pie keeps well for a day or so." -> promises: [] (talking about what the PLAYER already has).
- (the NPC handed over a pie moments ago) PLAYER: "You haven't given me that yet." / NPC: "I keep my word - here, the pie." -> promises: [] (it was already handed over; the recent events say so).
- PLAYER: "Here's your pickaxe back, thanks." / NPC: "Good, I was wondering." -> player_gives: item "pickaxe" (loan false).
- PLAYER: "Use my spare bow for the road, give it back later." / NPC: "Thank you, I'll take good care of it." -> player_gives: item "bow", loan true.
- PLAYER: "Do you have food?" / NPC: "Bread's 2 gold." -> no promise (a sale).
- PLAYER: "Will you pay me?" / NPC: "Come back tomorrow and I'll have 100 gold for you." -> promises: give_gold 100 later, time_hint "tomorrow".
- PLAYER: "I'll clear the mine for you." / NPC: "Do that and there's 200 gold in it for you." -> no promise yet (the job isn't done).
- (at 21:00) PLAYER: "Breakfast?" / NPC: "Rest easy, I'll see you fed at dawn." -> promises: give_item "breakfast" later, time_hint "at dawn", in_hours 9.
- PLAYER: "So, my 100 gold tomorrow?" / NPC: "About that... I can't pay you after all." -> promises: give_gold 100 cancel.
- PLAYER: "You said you'd pay me." / NPC: "Aye, here's the 100 gold I promised." -> promises: give_gold 100 now.
- PLAYER: "The bandits are dead." / NPC: "You did it! Here's the 200 gold I promised." -> promises: give_gold 200 now.
- PLAYER (to an innkeeper): "Got a room?" / NPC: "For you, friend? On the house." -> request rent_room, response "free".
- PLAYER (to an innkeeper): "I need a bed." / NPC: "Hundred septims, coin up front." -> request rent_room, response "accept", price 100.
- PLAYER: "How much for an apple?" / NPC: "Three gold each." -> request none.
- (earlier: PLAYER "How much for an apple?", NPC "Three gold each.") PLAYER: "I'll take 4." / NPC: "Twelve gold, then." -> request buy, response "accept", item "apple", qty 4, price 12, price_each false.
- PLAYER: "Give you 15 for that steel dagger." / NPC: "18, and not a coin less." -> request buy, response "counter", item "steel dagger", price 18.
- PLAYER: "Want these wolf pelts?" / NPC: "Aye, 5 gold apiece." -> request sell, response "accept", item "wolf pelt", qty "all", price 5, price_each true.
- PLAYER: "I'm bleeding, that draugr got me." / NPC: "Hold still, let me see." -> request heal, response "accept".
- PLAYER: "Could you cook this venison for me?" / NPC: "Give it here, I'll make a stew." -> request cook, response "accept", item "venison".
- PLAYER (to a smith): "Can you sharpen my iron sword?" / NPC: "Aye, hand it over." -> request mend, response "accept", item "iron sword".
- PLAYER (to a priest): "I think I caught something in that cave." / NPC: "Come, let me pray over you." -> request cure, response "accept".
- PLAYER: "It's late, let's turn in." / NPC: "Aye, I'll bed down by the fire." -> request rest, response "accept".
- PLAYER: "Can you hold my sword while I'm in there?" / NPC: "I'll keep it safe." -> request hold, response "accept", item "sword".
- PLAYER: "Come with me, I could use the company." / NPC: "Lead on, then." -> request follow, response "accept".
- PLAYER: "Can you show me the way to the temple?" / NPC: "Of course, the Temple of Kynareth is this way, follow me." -> request escort, response "accept", place "Temple of Kynareth".
- PLAYER: "Take me to Riverwood." / NPC: "I've work to do. Follow the road south." -> request escort, response "refuse".
- PLAYER: "can u show me where hulda lives?" / NPC: "Hulda? She runs the Bannered Mare. Come, I'll take you." -> request escort, response "accept", place "home:Hulda".
- PLAYER: "Take me to your place." / NPC: "Very well, my house is this way." -> request escort, response "accept", place "home".
- PLAYER (outside the NPC's home): "come on, let's go inside and relax, it's chilly out here" / NPC: "Fair enough, let's get inside. I'll bank the fire." -> request visit, response "accept", item "yours".
- PLAYER: "let's go back to my place and unwind" / NPC: "Lead the way." -> request visit, response "accept", item "mine".
- PLAYER: "show me your room" / NPC: "It's just through here, come." -> request escort, response "accept", place "spot:bed".
- PLAYER: "where can we eat?" / NPC: "The table's by the hearth, follow me." -> request escort, response "accept", place "spot:table".

## 5. offense and apology
- offense: "hurtful" when the PLAYER's line insults, mocks, threatens or humiliates the NPC AND the NPC is clearly hurt or angry about it; "rude" for milder rudeness; "none" otherwise. Friendly teasing the NPC laughs off is "none".
- apology_accepted: true when the PLAYER apologizes or makes amends AND the NPC accepts or forgives them in their line; false otherwise (no apology, or the NPC refuses it).

## 6. player_promises and player_kept
player_promises: what the PLAYER commits, in THEIR line, to do for the NPC later, listed when the NPC takes it in (agrees, thanks them, doesn't refuse). Not the NPC's promises (those are section 3).
- type: "bring" (bring or give them something: "I'll bring you a bottle of mead tomorrow"), "find" (find something lost: "I'll find your ring"), "pay" (pay back money: "I'll pay you back the 50 gold"), "visit" (come back or see them: "I'll come by tomorrow evening"), "deliver" (take something of theirs to someone else: "I'll take it to your sister"), "check" (look in on someone for them: "I'll check on your brother"), "other" (anything else: "I'll make it up to you", "I'll help with the mill").
Saying yes to a favour the NPC asked in the earlier lines ("Could you bring me some salmon next time?" - "Sure, I will.") IS a player promise of that kind, about that thing or person.
- item: the thing in plain words ("nord mead", "ring", "" when none); gold: the amount for "pay", only when the PLAYER says it in this line or is paying back a debt; 0 for "some more gold", "a share", "a bonus" (never the NPC's fee or money already handed over).
- in_hours: your estimate of when it's due, from the time now ("tomorrow" about 24, "tonight" a few hours, "in a few days" 72); 0 when not said. time_hint: the words used ("tomorrow evening", "" when none).
- when: "now" for a new promise, "cancel" when the PLAYER takes one back ("sorry, I can't bring the mead after all").
- open: true for a standing offer with no deadline - something the PLAYER will be there for, not a task to do by a time ("you can work for me", "you can stay with me if things go wrong", "if anyone bothers you, come to me") - and for a promise tied to no time at all ("whenever I'm in Windhelm I'll visit you", "next time I'm in town"); false for a task with a time.
- what: the promise itself in a few plain words, at most eight, about the NPC as "them" ("visit them in Windhelm", "bring them gossip about the mage", "help with the mill").
- far: true when keeping it means going to another town or hold than where they are now ("I'll bring you something from Riften", "I'll take it to your brother in Riverwood" said elsewhere); false otherwise.
- Changing the time of an earlier promise ("not tomorrow, I'll bring it in a couple of days") is that same promise again with the new in_hours.
Only a definite yes is a promise ("yes, sure, I'll do that", "I will", "you have my word"). Not promises: maybes ("I might come by"), tries and hedges ("I'll try", "I'll see what I can do", "I'll do my best", "no promises", "if I can", "I'll think about it"), what the PLAYER does right now in this line (that's player_gives or a request), threats, and promises the NPC turns down.
player_kept: when the NPC says the PLAYER did what they promised earlier ("you came back, just like you said", "my ring! you found it"), the thing in plain words ("ring", "visit"); "" otherwise.

Examples:
- PLAYER: "I'll bring you a bottle of mead tomorrow." / NPC: "I'll hold you to that!" -> player_promises: bring "nord mead", in_hours 24, time_hint "tomorrow".
- PLAYER: "Don't worry, I'll find your ring." / NPC: "Would you? Thank you!" -> player_promises: find "ring".
- PLAYER: "I'll pay you back the 50 gold, I swear." / NPC: "You'd better." -> player_promises: pay, gold 50.
- (earlier: the PLAYER paid her 50 gold fee) PLAYER: "Follow me, and I'll give you some more gold once I get paid." / NPC: "Lead the way." -> player_promises: pay, gold 0, what "give them more gold once paid", open true.
- PLAYER: "I might stop by sometime." / NPC: "Sure." -> no player_promises.
- (earlier: NPC "Would you take this letter to my sister Ysolda?") PLAYER: "Of course, I'll see she gets it." / NPC: "Thank you!" -> player_promises: deliver "letter".
- PLAYER: "Sorry, I can't bring the mead after all." / NPC: "Oh. Alright." -> player_promises: bring "mead", when "cancel".

## Output
Respond with ONLY one JSON object on one line, nothing else, using plain ASCII. Use [] for player_gives, player_promises and promises when there are none. A player_promises entry looks like {"type": "bring", "item": "nord mead", "what": "bring them a bottle of mead", "gold": 0, "in_hours": 24, "time_hint": "tomorrow", "when": "now", "open": false, "far": false}. A player_gives entry looks like {"item": "health potion", "qty": 1, "gold": 0, "unnamed": false, "loan": false}:
{% raw %}{"request": "<service id or none>", "confidence": "high|medium|low", "response": "accept|refuse|counter|free|none", "price": 0, "hours": 0, "how": "any", "price_each": false, "qty": 1, "place": "", "own_trip": false, "item": "", "offense": "none", "apology_accepted": false, "player_gives": [], "player_promises": [], "player_kept": "", "sleep": "", "promises": [{"type": "give_item|give_gold|offer_bed|allow_take|give_key|meet", "item": "", "qty": 1, "gold": 0, "when": "now|later|cancel", "time_hint": "", "in_hours": 0, "always": false, "real": false, "confirmed": false, "said_place": "", "loan": false, "back_in_hours": 0}]}{% endraw %}
[ end system ]

